@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-function-comparators Function Comparators
@brief Functions for comparing actual and expected values in tests.

@details
A check written as a plain boolean, such as `t.expect (x == 42)`, reports
only its location when it fails. The function comparators compare two
values and, when the check fails, also report both values:

| Comparator              | True when                          |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `mt::eq (a, b)`         | `a == b`                           |
| `mt::ne (a, b)`         | `a != b`                           |
| `mt::lt (a, b)`         | `a < b`                            |
| `mt::le (a, b)`         | `a <= b`                           |
| `mt::gt (a, b)`         | `a > b`                            |
| `mt::ge (a, b)`         | `a >= b`                           |
| `mt::approx (a, b, e)`  | `a` and `b` differ by at most `e`  |

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check various conditions", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer is 42";
  t.expect (mt::ne (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is not 43";
  t.expect (mt::lt (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is < 43";
  t.expect (mt::le (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is <= 43";
  t.expect (mt::gt (compute_answer (), 41)) << "answer is > 41";
  t.expect (mt::ge (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer is >= 42";
});
@endcode

When `compute_answer()` returns 41, the human-readable reporter displays
the failed checks with the compared values:

@code{.console}
    • Check various conditions
        ✗ answer is 42 FAILED (main.cpp:21, 41 == 42)
        ✗ answer is > 41 FAILED (main.cpp:25, 41 > 41)
        ✗ answer is >= 42 FAILED (main.cpp:26, 41 >= 42)
    ✗ Check various conditions - FAILED (3 checks passed, 3 failed)
@endcode

@par How the values are compared
- The operands must be comparable with the operator of the comparator;
  for example, `mt::eq (a, b)` does not compile when `a == b` would not.
- Integers of different signedness are compared by value, as by
  `std::cmp_equal()`: `mt::eq (-1, static_cast<unsigned> (-1))` fails,
  although `-1 == static_cast<unsigned> (-1)` is true.
- C strings (`char` arrays, including the string literals, and `char`
  pointers) are compared by content, also with `std::string` and
  `std::string_view`, so `mt::eq (actual, "abc")` checks the characters,
  not the addresses. A null `char` pointer is equal only to another null
  pointer (or `nullptr`), not to an empty string.
- Other arrays (including `uint8_t` buffers) are compared element by
  element, also with a container.
- Other pointers are compared by address.
- Values of the application types are displayed only if the application
  provides an `operator<<` for them; see
  @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-customisation.

@par Comparing approximately
The comparison of floating-point values with `eq()` is exact, so
`mt::eq (0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)` fails. To accept a difference, use
`mt::approx (a, b, epsilon)`, which is true when the operands differ by
at most `epsilon`, the limit included:
- the operands and the tolerance are integral or floating-point numbers,
  of any type; the distance of integers is computed exactly, also for
  operands of different signedness;
- a negative tolerance fails all the comparisons;
- a failed check shows the operands and the tolerance, as in
  `1.0 ~ (1.5 +/- 0.1)`.

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check approximate values", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::approx (read_adc (), 512, 3)) << "ADC reading";
  t.expect (mt::approx (compute_float (), 42.0f, 0.001f))
      << "result is about 42";
});
@endcode

@par Comparing containers
Containers are compared with their own operators, so they must be
comparable, usually by being of the same type. When the check fails, the
elements of both containers are shown, up to the first 16; when two
longer containers differ after them, the report adds the index of the
first difference.

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check containers", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (std::vector<int>{ 1, 2 }, std::vector<int>{ 1, 2 }))
      << "vector{ 1, 2 } eq vector{ 1, 2 }";

  t.expect (mt::ne (std::vector<int>{ 1, 2, 3 },
                    std::vector<int>{ 1, 2, 4 }))
      << "vector{ 1, 2, 3 } ne vector{ 1, 2, 4 }";
});
@endcode
